.TH WB 1 "" "watchbill"
.SH NAME
wb, watchbill \- a harness for AI agents in embedded software development
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwb\fR <command> [<args>]
\fBwb help\fR [<command>]
\fBwb \-\-version\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Watchbill is a harness for AI agents in embedded software development, deployed at a fixed revision onto a project that already exists. The command is wb, and watchbill is the same program.
.PP
wb help says the commands by group, and wb help <command> says more of one command. Each command has a page of its own, named wb\-<command>.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR wb\-init (1)
Set Watchbill up in this repository.
.TP
.BR wb\-check (1)
Say whether the repository is at its revision.
.TP
.BR wb\-upgrade (1)
Pin the repository to the installed revision.
.TP
.BR wb\-plan (1)
Take a plan in, and list, show, edit, accept or remove it.
.TP
.BR wb\-accept (1)
Accept the file of an issue.
.TP
.BR wb\-start (1)
Start a session of one role.
.TP
.BR wb\-status (1)
List the issues in the flow and where each stands.
.TP
.BR wb\-view (1)
Show what a session of a role sees.
.TP
.BR wb\-answer (1)
Answer the open questions of an issue.
.TP
.BR wb\-revert (1)
Take an issue back to before a step.
.TP
.BR wb\-prompt (1)
Print the prompt of a role.
.TP
.BR wb\-revision (1)
Print the version, the revision and its manifest.
.TP
.BR wb\-compare (1)
Say what changed between two revisions.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Every command uses the same statuses.
.TP
.B 0
It holds, or it is done.
.TP
.B 1
It does not hold, or it was refused.
.TP
.B 2
The command was used wrongly, or the repository is not set up.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .watchbill/watchbill.yml
The settings of the project.
.TP
.I /etc/watchbill/organisation.yml
The settings of the organisation.
.TP
.I /usr/share/watchbill
The files of the revision.
